course: dictionary
lesson: 4139
title: Words that show how someone feels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An adverb tells you how something is done. In «said gloomily», the word gloomily tells you how the person spoke. Look at the verb next to it: said, stared, sat. The adverb adds the feeling.
explain: «The March Hare dips it in his tea and gloomily repeats that it was the best butter.» Gloomily tells you the Hare is not happy as he repeats his words. Read the sentence with a sad, flat voice and the meaning becomes clear.
explain: Gloomily comes from gloomy, which means dark or sad. Adding -ly turns it into an adverb. In Toad's story, he sits gloomily in his cell before he cheers himself up, so the word shows his low mood at that moment.
fact: «Gloomily» means in a sad, hopeless or miserable way.
fact: «Gloomily» can also mean in a dark, dim way, but this meaning is rarer.
fact: Adverbs like gloomily often tell you how someone does an action, such as speaking, staring or sitting.
fact: Gloomy is the adjective, and gloomily is the adverb: the gloomy room, the room was lit gloomily.
fact: Words close to gloomily include miserably and sadly, but gloomily often suggests a quiet, heavy sadness.
exercise: «The March Hare dips it in his tea and gloomily repeats that it was the best butter.» How does the Hare repeat his words? | In a loud, cheerful way | In a sleepy, careless way | In a fast, excited way | *In a sad, hopeless way
exercise: «He sat gloomily by the window, staring at the rain.» What does gloomily tell you about him? | He was fast asleep | *He felt low and unhappy | He was dancing with joy | He was very hungry
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as gloomily? | Quickly | Loudly | *Miserably | Happily
test: Toad «sat gloomily in his prison cell» before he cheered himself up. What feeling does gloomily show? | Joyful and excited | *Low and unhappy | Very sleepy and calm | Angry and noisy
test: «The attic was lit gloomily by one dusty bulb, so the corners were hard to see.» What does gloomily mean here? | In a fast, careless way | In a happy, bright way | In a loud, noisy way | *In a dim, dark way
exam: Mia says gloomily is just a fancy word for sadly. Which answer is best? | Wrong, it means very fast | Right, and it has no other meaning at all | Wrong, it means very loud and cheerful | *Mostly right, but gloomily also means dim and dark, and it hints at a heavy, hopeless feeling
